    "Two things arrived in 13th-century Europe at almost the same moment, and the second nearly destroyed the first. The first was a huge, silent young man from a noble Italian family, so quiet and so heavily built that his fellow students at Cologne nicknamed him \"the dumb ox.\" The second was Aristotle — the complete works of the greatest mind of pagan antiquity, lost to the Latin West for five hundred years and now flooding back into the universities through Arabic translation, carrying conclusions that seemed to blow Christian theology apart at the seams.",
